2026-10-06 10:44:22 [simon@midnightblue.local] i would like to plan algorithm to chunk large log files into
2026-10-06 10:44:59 [simon@midnightblue.local] my ai harness, for continuity should i include overlap, should i
2026-10-06 10:45:15 [simon@midnightblue.local] keep summary growing in same context, what other questions should
2026-10-06 10:45:16 [simon@midnightblue.local] i have
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.004 684B 19.79s
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] A few direct answers first, then a checklist of questions that usually determine the design.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] • Should you include overlap?*
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] Usually yes, but selectively—not as a blanket fixed percentage.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - Best approach: chunk on natural boundaries first—time window, request/session ID, trace ID, or
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] complete event. If you do that, raw overlap becomes much less important.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - If you must split by token/line count, overlap by the last complete event or a small tail. Do not
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] split multiline stack traces or multi-line JSON.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - Prefer “summary overlap” over raw overlap: pass the previous chunk’s structured summary plus the
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] last complete event. That preserves continuity with far less token cost.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - If you do overlap raw text, deduplicate by event hash or ID. Otherwise repeated lines get
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] summarized twice and counts, error rates, and timelines become wrong.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - Overlap is most valuable when events span boundaries: stack traces, retries, sessions,
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] multi-service transactions.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] • Should you keep the summary growing in the same context?*
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] Generally no.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - A growing summary inside the same context will eventually bloat cost, dilute attention, and
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] increase drift. The model may also start treating old summary text as current fact.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - Use a bounded rolling summary stored outside the model context. For each chunk, send: system
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] instructions + current rolling summary + current chunk. Do not append every previous chunk and
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] summary indefinitely.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - Periodically re-compress the summary—e.g., every 5–10 chunks—into a tighter structured state.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - If you need long-term recall, store per-chunk summaries and raw chunk references in a database or
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] vector index, then retrieve when needed. That is usually better than one ever-growing context.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] • A practical chunking pattern*
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] 1. Parse logs into atomic events with metadata: timestamp, source, severity, trace/request ID, line
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] offsets, hash.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] 2. Segment into chunks by time window, event count, or token budget—but never split an atomic
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] event.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] 3. For each chunk:
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash]    - extract new facts, anomalies, errors, open items;
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash]    - update the rolling summary;
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash]    - store the chunk summary plus a pointer to the raw log range.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] 4. Maintain a structured continuity state, for example:
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash]    - time range covered;
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash]    - timeline of key events;
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash]    - open/resolved incidents;
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash]    - services/entities involved;
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash]    - error counts and first/last seen;
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash]    - unresolved questions;
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash]    - confidence and unknowns.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] 5. If processing in parallel, merge chunk summaries in order afterward. Sequential processing is
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] simpler for continuity.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] • Questions you should have*
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - What is the harness actually doing: summarization, incident detection, Q&A, root-cause analysis,
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] or agent memory?
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - What must never be lost: exact error messages, stack traces, correlation IDs, ordering, or
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] timestamps?
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - What is the natural unit of continuity: time, session, request, service, or incident?
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - What is the model context limit, and what is your per-chunk token budget and total cost budget?
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - Can you chunk on time windows, or must you chunk on tokens? Time windows often align better with
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] logs.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - How will you detect and handle a chunk boundary that splits an event?
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - How will you deduplicate overlapping events?
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - How will you merge conflicting or repeated summaries?
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - Do you need exact citations back to raw log lines? If yes, store offsets and line numbers.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - How will you handle out-of-order logs, time gaps, log rotation, and compressed files?
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - How will you redact secrets/PII before sending logs to the model?
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - How will you evaluate continuity? Build a test set of incidents or questions that span chunk
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] boundaries.
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - What happens when the summary drifts or becomes wrong? Can you re-baseline from raw chunks?
2026-10-06 10:45:36 [deepseek-flash] - Do you need retrieval across chunks, or only a global summary?
2026-10-06 10:45:37 [deepseek-flash] - How will retries and checkpoints work if a chunk fails mid-run?
2026-10-06 10:45:37 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:45:37 [deepseek-flash] If you share the log format and the harness goal, the chunk size, overlap strategy, and summary
2026-10-06 10:45:37 [deepseek-flash] schema can be made much more specific.
2026-10-06 10:45:37 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:56:36 [simon@midnightblue.local] currently i would like te feed 24MB of logs from the tool back into itself for summary of sessions
2026-10-06 10:57:19 [simon@midnightblue.local] pehaps a two pass algorithm could be good so entire logs are procesed twice?
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 5.066KB 8.61s
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] Two passes is a sound instinct, but the win comes from what each pass operates on, not from
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] re-reading the raw logs twice. Re-reading 24MB of raw text twice is usually the expensive,
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] low-value version.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] • Reality check on size*
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 24MB is roughly 5–7M tokens. That is several times any current context window, so chunking is
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] unavoidable, and a full raw second pass roughly doubles input cost. Before designing around that,
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] check whether you can shrink the input:
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Group by session ID first. If sessions are the unit of continuity, many will be short and need no
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] chunking at all. Only long sessions hit the chunking path.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Deduplicate by log template. Logs are highly repetitive; template extraction often cuts volume by
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 5–20x with no loss of signal.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Filter to severity/level or time window if the summary only needs notable events.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] If you can get from 24MB to ~2–4MB of distinct content, the whole problem gets easier and a second
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] pass becomes cheap.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] • Recommended two-pass shape*
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] Pass 1 — extract, don’t summarize prose.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Walk chunks in order, one per session (or per session segment).
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Emit structured records, not flowing text: timestamped events, errors with first/last seen,
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] counts, session boundaries, entities, open questions, and a pointer (file + byte/line offset) to
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] the raw range.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - This pass is deterministic-ish and cheap to validate.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] Pass 2 — synthesize per session.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Input is the structured records from pass 1, not the raw logs.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Produce the session summary, timeline, and open items.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - If a session’s records look ambiguous, contradictory, or high-severity, then do a
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] targeted raw re-read of just that session’s byte range. That gives you the benefit of a second raw
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] pass without paying for it everywhere.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] So: pass 1 over all raw (once), pass 2 over compressed artifacts, plus selective raw re-reads. That
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] is usually better than two full raw passes.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] • Where a full second raw pass is justified*
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] Only if you need continuity that the first pass cannot capture, for example:
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Later events change the meaning of earlier ones (retry storms, cascading failures).
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - You need cross-session correlation that only emerges once everything is seen.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] In that case, do pass 1 to build a skeleton/summary, then pass 2 re-reads raw chunks with the
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] skeleton as context. That is genuinely a second raw pass, but the context you supply should be
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] the compact skeleton, not the growing full history. Keep the injected context bounded.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] • Continuity mechanics that matter either way*
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Bounded rolling summary, re-compressed every N chunks. Do not let the summary grow unbounded in
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] context.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Overlap only at atomic-event boundaries (stack traces, multi-line JSON, sessions). Dedup
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] overlapping events by hash or event ID.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Store chunk outputs keyed by session and offset so you can re-run a single session without
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] reprocessing everything.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Keep an evaluation set of sessions whose summaries you can check, including ones that span chunk
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] boundaries.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] • Open questions specific to your case*
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - What defines a “session” in these logs — a session ID field, a time gap, a request chain, or
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] something inferred?
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Do you need exact citations back to raw lines, or is a prose summary enough?
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Is the second pass meant to improve accuracy, or just to roll up per-chunk summaries? Those are
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] different designs.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Can you run passes in parallel per session, or does ordering matter for your continuity?
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - What is your token/cost ceiling per run, and how often will this run?
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] - Do you have a known-good session summary to evaluate against?
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] If you tell me the log format (does it have session IDs? is it line-JSON? multiline stack traces?)
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] and the summary output you want, I can make the pass structure and chunk boundaries concrete.
2026-10-06 10:57:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:14 [simon@midnightblue.local] i will attach source of tool and then some examples logs
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.007 168.7KB 20.87s
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] Reading the source changes the answer, because your log format already gives you most of what
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] chunking algorithms usually have to invent.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] • What your format already provides*
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] Every line is `<8 hex slopmark> [source] content`. That gives you:
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - A parseable timestamp on every line (`parseInt(line.substring(0,8),16)`, sixteenths since
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 2025-04-12).
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - A source tag in brackets — `[roha]`, `[ASSISTANT]`, `[RELAY]`, `[GEMINI]`, `[CLAUDE]`, `[FORGE]`,
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] `[KOHA]`, `[FAIL]`, `[CRASH]`, `[SPEAK]`, `[TOOL]` — which separates user turns, model replies,
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] tool calls, tool results, and harness chatter.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - A session delimiter already in use: the bird-rune line `[roha] 𓅷 𓅸 𓅹 𓅺 𓅻 𓅼 𓅽`. Since
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] `logForge` appends, that line marks each process run in `forge.log`.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - A stronger unit you may not be using: `forge/transmission-.json`, written by `saveHistory`,
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] is `rohaHistory` verbatim — a structured per-session record with roles, names, tool_calls, prices,
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] elapsed. If your goal is session* summary, that is a far better input than re-parsing the raw
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] log.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] So the first design question is really: are you summarizing the conversation (use the
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] transmission JSON), or the harness behaviour (use the raw log — crashes, retries, model
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] resets, credit accounting)?
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] • Chunking algorithm for your format*
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] Do not chunk by bytes or tokens. Segment by the structure that is already there:
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 1. Session = lines between bird-rune markers. This is your top-level chunk. Sessions never
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] need to share context across the boundary, so no raw overlap is required at this level.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 2. Turn = a `[roha]` line that is a user message, through to the next such line. A turn
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] contains the user prompt, one or more `[ASSISTANT]` / model replies, `[RELAY]` tool calls, `[TOOL]`
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] results, and any `[FAIL]` / `[CRASH]` that occurred. Turns are atomic — never split one.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 3. Sub-turn split only if a single turn exceeds your token budget. Split at `[RELAY]`
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] tool-call boundaries or at blank-line paragraph breaks inside an assistant message. Never split a
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] stack trace, a fenced code block, or a multi-line JSON blob.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 4. Noise filter first. Drop `[DEBUG]`, `[TABLE]`, `[UNICODE]`, `[GETDATE]`, `[TEST2-4]`,
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] `[MULTI]`, `[STRIP]`, `[LOG]` chatter unless you specifically need it. On a typical 24MB forge.log
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] this alone removes a large fraction of bytes without losing signal.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] • Two-pass, tuned to this*
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] Pass 1 — deterministic, no model call. Walk the log once, emit one record per session:
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - `sessionId` (hash of first slopmark + byte offset)
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - `startSlopmark`, `endSlopmark`, `byteRange` in the source file
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - `sourceCounts` — a `{}` like your existing `days[date].tags`
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - `turns[]` — each with `userSlopmark`, `userText`, `assistantReplies[]`, `toolCalls[]`,
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] `toolResults[]`, `errors[]`
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - `modelSwitches[]` — every `[RELAY] model reset` line
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - `failures[]` — `[FAIL]`, `[CRASH]`, `unhandled error`, `maximum prompt length`, the specific
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] error strings your `relay()` catch block matches on
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - `shares[]` — paths from `[KOHA] Share file path:` lines
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - `pricing` — sum of any `$` amounts you log
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] This pass replaces and cleans up the logic already sitting in `readHistory` and `stripLog`. Those
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] two duplicate parsing, and `readHistory` has a few sharp edges worth fixing while you are in there:
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] the `if(!min || secs<min)` guard breaks on a legitimate `secs==0`, and the session/tags push
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] ordering only works because the bird line happens to start the next session rather than end the
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] previous one.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] Pass 2 — per session, one model call. Feed the session record, or the raw session lines if they fit
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] comfortably. Ask for a bounded summary against a fixed schema (timeline, decisions, unresolved
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] items, failures, entities, open questions). Store it keyed by `sessionId`.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] Pass 3 — optional roll-up. Feed the session summaries (not raw logs) plus a compact global
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] timeline into one call. This is where you get cross-session continuity cheaply.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] • Overlap*
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] Not needed at session boundaries, and not needed at turn boundaries, because both are
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] self-contained. Raw overlap is only justified when you must split mid-turn on a token budget, and
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] even then, overlap by the last complete tool-call/tool-result pair, not by a percentage of lines.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] Deduplicate by the 8-hex slopmark plus source tag, which is unique enough for a line.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] • Growing summary*
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] No. Keep the rolling summary bounded and outside the model context. For each session summarization
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] call, inject a fixed-size context: the previous two or three session summaries plus the current
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] session record. Re-compress every N sessions. Store all per-session summaries in `forge/` next to
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] `transmission-*.json` so a single session can be re-run without touching the other 23MB.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] • Questions worth answering before you build*
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - Is the summary target the conversation (`transmission-*.json`) or the harness log (crashes, model
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] resets, retries)? The two produce very different pass-1 records.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - Do you need to distinguish the user's `[roha]` lines from the harness's `[roha]` status lines?
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] Right now both share the tag; the id passed to `logForge` (`rohaUser` vs `"roha"`) is the only
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] discriminator, and it is not written into the line.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - What is the token budget per session call, and what is the model you plan to run pass 2 on? The
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] `defaultModel` and the cheap models in `modelSpecs` will produce very different quality on dense
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] log text.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - Do you need citations back to line offsets, or is a prose summary enough? If citations matter,
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] the `byteRange` in the pass-1 record is what makes it cheap.
2026-10-06 10:58:40 [deepseek-flash] - How do you evaluate continuity? A held-out set of sessions whose summaries you have already
