2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] Two things to get out of the way first: the top two lines are paste artefacts, not log lines.
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] `source env3/bin/activateyolo task=obb ...` and `oops` have no 8-hex prefix and no `[source]` tag,
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] so they are your shell prompt/history bleeding into the copy. Your parser should reject any line
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] that does not match `^[0-9a-f]{8} \[[^\]]+\] `. That single rule also drops the wrapped fragments
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] you can see mid-paste like `a full raw seco\nnd pass` — actually no, that one is a real log line,
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] it just lost its prefix in the paste. Which brings up the first correction.
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] • Correction 1: the slopmark is not a line identifier*
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] I suggested earlier that you could dedupe overlapping lines by slopmark plus source tag. That was
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] wrong. `logForge` computes `time` once per call and applies it to every split line, and `flush`
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] calls `logForge` once per `printBuffer` entry. So a single assistant message is dozens of log lines
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] that share one slopmark, and the slopmark drifts mid-message when the flush crosses a 62.5 ms tick
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] — you can see that in your sample: `2a2e3486` for the status and first content line, then
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] `2a2e3487`, `2a2e3488`, `2a2e3489` as the reply wraps. The slopmark is a flush timestamp, not a
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] record key. If you need a per-line key, use `(slopmark, source, ordinal within source run)`.
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] • Correction 2: the log is not a faithful copy of the conversation*
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] `mdToAnsi` consumes code-fence markers and emits ANSI background codes for code blocks; `logForge`
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] then strips ANSI. So in `forge.log` a fenced code block becomes unmarked indented-looking text with
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] no delimiters, and word wrap has already broken lines at 100 columns. That means reconstructing the
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] assistant message from the log is lossy for anything code-heavy. `forge/transmission-*.json` does
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] not have this problem. If your summary target is the conversation, parse the transmission JSON. Use
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] `forge.log` only for harness behaviour: shares, model switches, failures, cost, credit.
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] • Correction 3: the status line is a free structured record*
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] `2a2e3486 [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 5.066KB
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] 8.61s`
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] That is `rohaTitle`, `rohaModel`, provider emoji, forge glyph, temperature, cost, history size,
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] elapsed. The `🪠` means tools were off for that turn (`🐸` would mean tools on but functions
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] failed, the pail would mean active). Pass 1 should parse every line matching `^#🟠.*nitrologic
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] Relay` into a numeric turn record. You get per-turn spend, latency, and context size with no model
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] call at all.
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] • What the sample already tells you about your own problem*
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] The two status lines read `5.066KB` and `168.7KB`. That jump is the `relay.ts` share (137044 bytes)
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] plus the previous reply. The history is growing exactly the way you were worried about in your
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] earlier question, and it is visible in the log. The `maximum prompt length exceeded` path in your
2026-10-06 11:00:51 [deepseek-flash] catch block is the eventual outcome. A bounded per-session summary stored outside `rohaHistory` is
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] the fix, and the status line gives you the metric to watch it.
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] • Pass-1 rules that fall out of the sample*
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] Segment by source-run, not by line count. A turn is:
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] - one or more consecutive `[simon@midnightblue.local]` lines — these are the user's input lines,
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] joined with `\r\n` before relay, but logged individually with their own slopmarks;
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] - then a run of `[deepseek-flash]` lines whose first member matches the status pattern, followed by
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] content lines to be joined in order after stripping the leading `#`;
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] - any `[roha]`, `[FAIL]`, `[CRASH]`, `[TOOL]`, `[RELAY]` lines interleaved belong to the same turn;
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] - the turn ends at the next user line or the bird-rune session marker.
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] Session boundary is still `[roha] 𓅷 𓅸 𓅹 𓅺 𓅻 𓅼 𓅽`, which your `readHistory` already keys on.
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] Nothing in the sample crosses one, so this is a single open session.
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] • One question this raises*
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] Do you want pass 1 to read `forge.log`, the transmission JSON, or both joined on slopmark range?
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] The transmission JSON has clean message boundaries and preserves code fences; the log has harness
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] events the JSON does not (shares, model resets, credit deltas, the status metrics). If you want
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] both in one record, the join key is the session's slopmark range, which the bird markers give you.
2026-10-06 11:00:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:04:18 [simon@midnightblue.local] cd nitrologic.github.io
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.015 191.8KB 44.68s
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] refine.ts is already the pass-1 shape I described — deterministic, single pass, no model calls — so
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] the useful thing is to say where it diverges from what a pass-2 summarizer needs, and which parts
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] are quietly wrong.
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] • Bugs that will bite*
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 1. `reduce` is declared `async` and returns `Promise<number>`, but nothing inside awaits anything,
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] and the caller does not await it. So `count` in the per-file `console.log` prints a Promise. The
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] `Deno.writeTextFile` inside is also unawaited, so writes race with the loop and with the final
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] summary print. Either make the function synchronous or await both.
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 2. `history/` is never created. There is no `mkdir` anywhere in the file. If the directory is
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] missing, every write throws into the catch, you log `[REFINE] error`, and you produce nothing while
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] appearing to succeed.
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 3. Session detection keys on the literal substring `[roha] [FORGE]`, which only appears when
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] `echoInfo` is on (verbose or debugging) or when the model list changes. In the log sample you
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] shared there is no such line at all, so `sessionCount` would be 0 for that run. That cascades:
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] `stats.recent!=sessionCount` is `0!=0`, so the per-model `sessions` counter never increments and
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] the model histogram reports `sessions:0` for every model. The reliable marker is already in the log
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] — the bird-rune line emitted once by `echo(birds)` at startup, which is also what `readHistory`
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] keys on.
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 4. The failure branch in `parseHeader` is dead. `if(words[1]=="failure")` expects the second word
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] to be "failure", but the only failure line emitted is `[FORGE] enumerateModels endpoint failure for
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] account X`, so `words[0]` is "enumerateModels" and `words[1]` is "endpoint". No vendor failures are
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] ever recorded, which makes the later `maxTime==0` cleanup moot.
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 5. The `Connected to` parse is brittle. The line is `Connected to deepseek (13) 0.48s`, so
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] `parseInt("(13)")` is NaN. It happens to be unused, but the parsing is one edit away from a silent
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] error.
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 6. `minDate`/`maxDate` only update for kept lines, because `slopDate` is called after the filters.
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] Blanks, `[roha]`, `[remote]`, `[PORT]` and `[FAIL]` lines do not contribute. The reported range is
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] therefore narrower than the log, and if a run ends on a crash the end time is wrong.
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 7. `bigCount` is incremented and never reported; the `continue` silently drops the line.
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] • What it does not yet do, for feeding a model*
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] It flattens but does not rejoin. A single assistant reply is dozens of log lines, each with its own
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] slopmark and its own output record, so your history file presents each 100-column fragment as a
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] separate event. Pass 2 will read a paragraph as a dozen unrelated observations. The join rule is:
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] within one source tag, a status line starts a new message and everything from the same source until
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] the next status line or source change belongs to it. Consecutive `[simon@...]` lines are one
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] prompt.
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] It drops the slopmark. The output keeps a formatted date but not the raw sixteenths, which loses
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] sub-second ordering and the only cheap rejoin key you have. The `#` prefix that `logForge` adds to
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] model output is a cleaner discriminator than the current `@`/`:` heuristic, and it survives into
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] `trim2`.
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] It ignores the status line. `#🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 5.066KB
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 8.61s` is a complete per-turn record — model, provider, tools state, temperature, cost, context
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] size, latency. Parsing it gives you a numeric timeline with no model call, and it is exactly the
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] metric that shows the context growing from 5KB to 169KB in your sample before the
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] maximum-prompt-length path fires.
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] It emits one flat file per input file. For pass 2 you want one record per session, keyed by a
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] stable id derived from the start slopmark and the source file, so a single bad summary can be
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] regenerated without re-reading the whole corpus.
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] • One scoping question*
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] refine.ts lives in `nitrologic.github.io` and writes to `history/`, which looks like a publication
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] pipeline rather than a summarization pipeline. If that is right, leave it alone and add a separate
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] pass-1 that emits structured per-session records for the model. Publication wants readable prose;
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] pass 2 wants records, offsets, and rejoin keys. Trying to serve both from one output is what will
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] make the summarizer's input awkward.
2026-10-06 11:05:17 [deepseek-flash] 
