2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] You're right, my earlier answer only covered manual extended thinking with `budget_tokens`. Your
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] snippet uses adaptive thinking, which works differently:
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] 
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] - `thinking: {type: "adaptive"}` lets the model decide whether and how much to think for
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] each request, so you don't set a token budget. `budget_tokens` doesn't apply in this mode.
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] - `output_config: {effort: "high"}` guides how thoroughly Claude reasons and responds.
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] Higher effort generally means more thinking and more output tokens, so it affects cost and latency.
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] 
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] For logging, the same principle holds: capture any `thinking` blocks from the response along with
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] the request ID and final text. With adaptive thinking, the amount of reasoning varies per request,
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] so your logs may show thinking on some calls and little or none on others. Also check whether
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] thinking content is returned in full or summarized for the model you're using, since that
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] determines what you can actually log.
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] 
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] Support for `adaptive` and the `effort` option depends on the model, and these parameters have
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] changed across releases. I'd confirm the exact field names and supported values against Anthropic's
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] current extended thinking and effort documentation before relying on them.
2026-10-23 09:47:42 [claude-haiku-5-5] 
