2026-10-23 10:53:21 [simon@midnightblue.local] why is sky blue?
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 claude-haiku-5-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.001 495B 3.26s
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] The sky looks blue because of Rayleigh scattering.
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] 
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] Sunlight contains all colors of the visible spectrum. As it enters the atmosphere, it collides with
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] gas molecules, mainly nitrogen and oxygen, which are much smaller than the wavelength of light.
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] These molecules scatter shorter wavelengths (blue and violet) much more strongly than longer ones
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] (red and orange). This is because scattering intensity scales roughly with the inverse fourth power
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] of the wavelength.
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] 
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] So blue light gets scattered in all directions across the sky, and that scattered light reaches
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] your eyes from every part of the sky, making it look blue. Violet is scattered even more than blue,
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] but the sun emits less violet, and our eyes are less sensitive to it, so the sky appears blue
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] rather than violet.
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] 
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] At sunset, sunlight travels through much more atmosphere to reach you. Most of the blue has been
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] scattered away by then, leaving the reds and oranges.
2026-10-23 10:53:25 [claude-haiku-5-5] 
