2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 claude-haiku-5-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.002 610B 5.63s
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] Thanks for testing this. An empty `thinking` string in the block doesn't necessarily mean something
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] is broken, but a few things commonly cause it:
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] 
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] 1. Streaming accumulation. When streaming, the `content_block_start` event for a thinking
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] block usually carries `thinking: ""`. The actual text arrives afterward in `content_block_delta`
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] events with `thinking_delta` payloads. If the harness reads the field from the start event and
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] never appends the deltas, it will see an empty string even though the content streamed correctly.
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] 
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] 2. Thinking not enabled for the request. If the extended thinking parameter wasn't sent, or
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] the budget was set too low, the model may return little or no thinking text.
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] 
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] 3. Redacted thinking. Some blocks come back as `redacted_thinking` with an encrypted `data`
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] field instead of readable text. A harness expecting `thinking` text may treat these as empty.
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] 
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] 4. Signature-only blocks. A thinking block can include a `signature` with an empty or
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] minimal `thinking` field, particularly in multi-turn flows where earlier blocks are passed back.
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] 
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] To narrow it down, check the raw event stream or non-streamed JSON for the block type, whether
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] `thinking` is empty in the raw payload or only in your parsed output, and whether a `signature` is
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] present. If the raw payload is empty too, the issue is likely on the request side. If only your
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] parsed output is empty, the issue is probably in the accumulation logic.
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] 
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] I don't have direct access to my own reasoning content, so I can't confirm what the block should
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] contain from my side. If you share the raw block (with anything sensitive removed) or your request
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] parameters, I can help you work out which case you're hitting.
2026-10-23 11:59:12 [claude-haiku-5-5] 
