2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] SDK, depending on which Gemini model you are using.
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] 1. Native Thinking Mode (Gemini 2.0 and newer)
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] Google has introduced native "thinking" models (such as `gemini-2.0-flash-thinking-exp`) designed
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] specifically for complex reasoning. With these models, the reasoning process is enabled by default.
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] You can also control whether the thought process is returned in the API response using the
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] `thinkingConfig` parameter inside your generation configuration.
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] When enabled, the model returns its reasoning as a separate part of the response payload, marked
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] with a `thought` boolean property, alongside the final answer.
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] 2. Chain-of-Thought Prompting (Standard Models)
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] If you are using standard models (like `gemini-1.5-pro` or `gemini-1.5-flash`), there is no API
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] toggle for reasoning. Instead, you can achieve this by using "Chain-of-Thought" prompting.
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] You can instruct the model via the `systemInstruction` property—or by prepending text to your user
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] prompt—to explicitly state its reasoning step-by-step before providing the final conclusion. Asking
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] the model to enclose its thoughts in specific tags (such as `<thinking>...</thinking>`) makes it
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] easy to separate the reasoning from the final output in your application.
2026-10-23 12:47:19 [gemini-4-argon] 
