2026-10-31 11:13:12 [simon@midnightblue.local] in a bash sh, is there a simple way to calulate elapsed time when testing a command call
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.6 gemini-4-argon 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.000 661B 7.99s
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] Yes, there are a few simple ways to calculate elapsed time in Bash depending on your exact needs:
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] • 1. The `time` keyword*
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] For the quickest check, simply prepend the word `time` before your command. When the command
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] finishes, Bash will automatically print the real (total elapsed wall-clock time),
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] user (CPU time spent in user-mode), and sys (CPU time spent in kernel-mode) times
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] to standard error.
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] • Pros: Built-in, offers millisecond precision, requires no setup.
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] • Tip: You can customize its output format by setting the `TIMEFORMAT` environment variable
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] before running it.
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] • 2. The `$SECONDS` variable*
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] Bash has a built-in internal variable called `SECONDS` that tracks the number of whole seconds
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] since the shell (or script) was started. If you reset `SECONDS=0` right before your command and
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] read `$SECONDS` right after, it will contain the elapsed time.
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] • Pros: Very easy to capture the value into a variable or log file for later use in a
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] script.
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] • Cons: Only measures in whole seconds (no millisecond precision).
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] • 3. `EPOCHREALTIME` (Bash 5.0+)*
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] If you need to store the elapsed time in a variable and require sub-second precision, Bash
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] 5 introduced `EPOCHREALTIME`, which gives you the current Unix epoch time in microseconds. You can
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] capture this value before and after your command, and then pass the values to a tool like `bc` or
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] `awk` to subtract them.
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] • 4. The `date` command*
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] In older shells or strict POSIX `sh` environments where Bash-specific variables aren't available,
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] the traditional method is calling the external `date` utility before and after the command (using
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] `+%s` for seconds or `+%s.%N` on GNU systems for nanoseconds) and subtracting the start time from
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] the end time.
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] If you would like to see code examples or a reusable wrapper function for any of these approaches,
2026-10-31 11:13:20 [gemini-4-argon] please let me know.
