2026-10-31 11:16:57 [simon@midnightblue.local] time works well, is there an option to just emit real and not user and sys?
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.6 gemini-4-argon 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.001 2.803KB 5.50s
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] Yes, you can do this by setting the `TIMEFORMAT` environment variable in Bash.
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] By default, `time` prints real, user, and sys, but if you set `TIMEFORMAT` to `%R` (or `%3R` for
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] three decimal places, which is the default precision), it will only output the real elapsed time in
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] seconds.
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] Here are the main format specifiers you can assign to `TIMEFORMAT` for real time:
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] • `%R`: Prints the elapsed time in seconds and milliseconds (e.g., `1.234`).
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] • `%lR`: Prints the elapsed time in a longer `minutes` and `seconds` format (e.g.,
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] `0m1.234s`), which matches the standard `time` output style.
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] • `%0R`: Prints only whole seconds, omitting the decimal places.
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] You can also include custom text in the variable (for example, setting `TIMEFORMAT="Elapsed: %R
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] seconds"`). Once set in your script or terminal session, every subsequent call to `time` will
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] strictly use that format.
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] If you would like to see a syntax example of how to set this globally or scope it to a single
2026-10-31 11:17:03 [gemini-4-argon] command, just let me know.
