2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] In `bash` and `sh`, you assign variables directly using the `=` sign without spaces, rather than
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] using the `set` command (which is used in `csh` or `fish`).
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] Here is what that looks like in a script, along with a reusable function if you want to keep the
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] formatting scoped only to specific tests:
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] #!/usr/bin/env bash
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] # 1. Setting it globally for the session or script
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] TIMEFORMAT='%R'
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] time sleep 1
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] # 2. Or as a reusable function to avoid changing global settings
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] measure_real_time() {
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 	local TIMEFORMAT='%R'
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 	time "$@"
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] }
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] measure_real_time sleep 1
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] • A quick note on `sh` vs `bash`:*
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] `TIMEFORMAT` is a Bash-specific feature. If you run your script strictly with POSIX `sh` (for
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] example, `sh script.sh` on Ubuntu where `sh` is `dash`), `TIMEFORMAT` will be ignored. In strict
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] `sh`, you would instead call the external GNU time binary directly:
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] #!/bin/sh
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] measure_real_time_sh() {
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 	command time -f '%e' "$@"
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] }
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] measure_real_time_sh sleep 1
2026-10-31 11:18:02 [gemini-4-argon] 
