2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] Depending on which exact phase of the lifecycle you are referring to, there are a few standard
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] terms in machine learning:
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] • 1. After Initial Pre-training (The most common context)*
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] When a base model has finished its initial training (where it learns general patterns and its
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] foundational weights are established) and is trained further for specific tasks or behaviors, it is
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] called:
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] • Post-training: The broad umbrella term for any training done after the base pre-training
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] phase is complete.
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] • Fine-tuning: Taking a pre-trained model and training it further on a smaller, specific
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] dataset to adapt it to a specialized domain or task (often called SFT, or Supervised
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] Fine-Tuning).
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] • Alignment / RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback): Training the model after
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] initial weight calculation to ensure its outputs match human preferences, safety guidelines, and
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] instruction-following behavior.
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] • Continued (or Continual) Pre-training: Feeding a pre-trained model a large volume of new,
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] unlabeled data (such as a specific company's internal documents or a new language) before
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] fine-tuning it.
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] • Transfer Learning: The overarching concept of taking weights calculated for one task and
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] using them as the starting point for a different task.
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] • 2. During the Training Loop (After Weight Initialization)*
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] If you mean the step immediately following Weight Initialization (where initial starting
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] weights are calculated randomly or via algorithms like Xavier/He initialization before the model
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] has seen any data), the process is simply called:
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] • Optimization or Model Fitting: The iterative process of updating those initial
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] weights using Backpropagation and Gradient Descent across multiple epochs.
2026-10-31 12:11:44 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 12:12:53 [simon@midnightblue.local] name for cruft after distilling alcohol or making wine
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.6 gemini-4-argon 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.004 9.411KB 7.86s
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] Here are the specific terms for the leftover sediment, solids, and waste in winemaking, brewing,
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] and distilling:
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] • In Winemaking*
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] • Lees: The primary term for the sediment that settles at the bottom of the fermentation
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] vessel, consisting primarily of dead yeast cells and tiny grape particles.
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] • Pomace (or Marc): The solid remains of the grapes (skins, pulp, seeds, and stems)
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] left over right after pressing the juice.
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] • Must: The freshly pressed grape juice that still contains all the skins, seeds, and stems
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] before fermentation separates them out.
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] • In Distilling*
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] • Stillage (or Slop / Spent Wash): The acidic liquid and solid residue left
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] behind in the bottom of the still after the alcohol has been boiled off.
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] • Dunder: The specific term used in rum production for the yeast-rich, liquid residue left
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] in the still after distilling cane juice or molasses.
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] • Pot Ale: Used in malt whisky distilling for the copper-rich residue left at the bottom of
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] the first distillation (the wash still).
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] • Foreshots, Heads, and Tails (or Feints): The impure liquid fractions that
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] come off the still at the very beginning and very end of a run, which are separated from the
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] drinkable "heart."
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] • In Brewing (Beer & Mash)*
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] • Trub: The layer of heavy sediment (fats, proteins, hop residue, and inactive yeast) left
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] at the bottom of the fermenter or kettle.
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] • Draff (or Spent Grain): The exhausted grain solids left over after mashing
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] (extracting the sugars with hot water).
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] 
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] • General / Literary Terms*
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] • Dregs: The most common everyday term for the last, unwanted bits of sediment at the
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] bottom of a barrel, bottle, or glass.
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] • Barm: The thick, foamy yeast scum that rises to the top of fermenting liquor or
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] beer.
2026-10-31 12:13:03 [gemini-4-argon] 